WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.467
Exercises turn continuity plans into operational capability.

00:00:04.467 --> 00:00:12.086
Tabletop exercises let teams practice decisions, roles, escalation, and communications without affecting production.

00:00:12.086 --> 00:00:21.282
Scenarios should challenge assumptions such as unavailable staff, corrupted backups, failed communications, or delayed vendor support.

00:00:21.282 --> 00:00:34.683
Operational drills test specific actions: switching to alternate communications, locating offline procedures, starting backup power, moving to degraded mode, or coordinating a controlled shutdown.

00:00:34.683 --> 00:00:39.675
Scope drills carefully with safety and engineering approval.

00:00:39.675 --> 00:00:46.769
Recovery testing proves that backups, spares, procedures, credentials, and dependencies work together.

00:00:46.769 --> 00:00:52.812
Measure actual time, data loss, manual effort, and validation steps.

00:00:52.812 --> 00:01:00.432
Testing isolated components is useful, but end-to-end exercises reveal sequencing and ownership gaps.

00:01:00.432 --> 00:01:06.475
Assign a plan owner and owners for every recovery capability.

00:01:06.475 --> 00:01:15.146
Maintain contact lists, architecture diagrams, vendor information, spare inventories, software media, licenses, and recovery sites.

00:01:15.146 --> 00:01:20.663
Review after personnel, process, equipment, network, or supplier changes.

00:01:20.663 --> 00:01:28.808
Integrate safety, operations, engineering, maintenance, cybersecurity, IT, facilities, communications, legal, leadership, and relevant vendors.

00:01:28.808 --> 00:01:33.275
Each group sees different consequences and dependencies.

00:01:33.275 --> 00:01:38.267
Governance should resolve decision authority before an emergency.

00:01:38.267 --> 00:01:45.887
After every exercise and incident, record lessons, assign actions, fund improvements, and retest.

00:01:45.887 --> 00:01:55.608
Useful metrics include recovery success, achieved objectives, untested assets, stale documentation, unavailable contacts, and overdue corrective work.

00:01:55.608 --> 00:02:03.228
A continuity program remains credible only when it is practiced, maintained, and improved.
